Protocol: PhotoPrint_W2

# Week 2 — first AnB version of the PhotoPrint protocol.
#
# Simplifications applied (as permitted for week 2):
#   - A has a public/private key pair (no identity provider yet).
#   - Every agent knows the public key of every other agent.
#   - Authorization is modelled as A directly issuing a signed
#     grant token to P, which P then presents to B.
#   - The resource (photos) is modelled as a fresh Number PhotosA
#     known only to B initially.
#
# The identity-provider-based authentication is introduced in w3.
#
# Agents:  A (resource owner), P (print service), B (photo storage)
#
# Fresh values (not in initial knowledge):
#   Na       — nonce from A, binds the grant to this session
#   Np       — nonce from P, allows B to bind its response
#   PhotosA  — the photos resource held by B

Types:
    Agent A, P, B;
    Number Na, Np, PhotosA;
    Function photos

Knowledge:
    A: A, P, B, pk(A), inv(pk(A)), pk(P), pk(B);
    P: A, P, B, pk(A), pk(P), inv(pk(P)), pk(B);
    B: A, P, B, pk(A), pk(P), pk(B), inv(pk(B)), photos(A)

where A!=P, B!=P

Actions:
    # Step 1: P -> A  (access request)
    # P asks A to authorise access to her photos at B.
    # Signed by P so A can verify the requester; encrypted for A.
    P -> A: {A, P, B, Np}pk(A)

    # Step 2: A -> P  (authorisation grant)
    # A issues a signed grant allowing P to access photos(A) at B.
    # Na is a fresh nonce from A binding this grant to the session.
    # Signed by A so B can verify A's authorisation; encrypted for P.
    A -> P: {{A, P, B, Na, Np}inv(pk(A))}pk(P)

    # Step 3: P -> B  (resource request with grant)
    # P presents A's signed grant to B together with a fresh nonce Np2
    # so B can bind its response to this specific request.
    # Signed by P and encrypted for B.
    P -> B: {{A, P, B, Na, Np}inv(pk(A)), Np}pk(B)

    # Step 4: B -> P  (photos delivery)
    # B verifies A's signature on the grant and returns the photos.
    # Encrypted for P; Na included to bind to the original grant.
    B -> P: {photos(A), Na, Np}pk(P)


Goals:
    photos(A) secret between B, P;

    P authenticates A on A, P, B, Na, Np
    B authenticates A on A, P, B, Na, Np
    P authenticates B on photos(A), Na, Np


# =============================================================================
# Special Task – Week 2: Static Analysis (Passive Intruder)
# =============================================================================
#
# Setting: all four roles (A=a, P=p, B=b) run honestly for one session.
# The intruder i only OBSERVES network traffic — it never injects, replays,
# or modifies messages. We apply the Dolev-Yao analysis procedure to the
# set of messages the intruder sees on the wire, and determine which terms
# it can derive.
#
# --- Messages on the network (one honest session) ---
#
# Let the concrete values be:
#   na  = value of Na  (fresh nonce chosen by a)
#   np  = value of Np  (fresh nonce chosen by p)
#   ph  = value of photos(a)
#
# msg1 = {a, p, b, np}pk(a)                        [Step 1, P->A]
# msg2 = {{a, p, b, na, np}inv(pk(a))}pk(p)        [Step 2, A->P]
# msg3 = {{a, p, b, na, np}inv(pk(a)), np}pk(b)    [Step 3, P->B]
# msg4 = {ph, na, np}pk(p)                         [Step 4, B->P]
#
# --- Intruder's initial knowledge ---
#
# M0 = { a, p, b, i, pk(a), pk(p), pk(b), pk(i), inv(pk(i)) }
#
# The intruder knows all public keys (Dolev-Yao assumption) and its own
# private key. It does NOT know inv(pk(a)), inv(pk(p)), inv(pk(b)), na, np,
# or photos(a) at the start.
#
# --- Analysis procedure ---
#
# We start from M0 and apply the analysis steps to each observed message:
#
#   Step A1 – analyse msg1 = {a, p, b, np}pk(a):
#     DecAsym: need M ⊢c pk(a)? No (pk(a) ∈ M0 but we need inv(pk(a))).
#     => msg1 CANNOT be decrypted.  No new terms.
#
#   Step A2 – analyse msg2 = {{a, p, b, na, np}inv(pk(a))}pk(p):
#     DecAsym: need inv(pk(p)) — not in M0.
#     => msg2 CANNOT be decrypted.  No new terms.
#
#   Step A3 – analyse msg3 = {{a, p, b, na, np}inv(pk(a)), np}pk(b):
#     DecAsym: need inv(pk(b)) — not in M0.
#     => msg3 CANNOT be decrypted.  No new terms.
#
#   Step A4 – analyse msg4 = {ph, na, np}pk(p):
#     DecAsym: need inv(pk(p)) — not in M0.
#     => msg4 CANNOT be decrypted.  No new terms.
#
# After exhausting all messages: M_final = M0  (no new terms added).
#
# --- What the passive intruder learns ---
#
# From the network traffic the intruder observes four ciphertexts, but
# cannot decrypt any of them because:
#   - msg1 is encrypted with pk(a): requires inv(pk(a)), which only a holds.
#   - msg2 is encrypted with pk(p): requires inv(pk(p)), which only p holds.
#   - msg3 is encrypted with pk(b): requires inv(pk(b)), which only b holds.
#   - msg4 is encrypted with pk(p): requires inv(pk(p)), which only p holds.
#
# The intruder learns ONLY what it already knew: the agent names and public
# keys in M0, plus the four ciphertext blobs as opaque terms (but it cannot
# decompose them).
#
# In particular the intruder CANNOT derive:
#   - na  (nonce of a)            — inside msg1, msg3; both undecryptable
#   - np  (nonce of p)            — inside msg2, msg3, msg4; all undecryptable
#   - photos(a)                   — inside msg4; undecryptable
#   - {a,p,b,na,np}inv(pk(a))    — the inner signed blob in msg2/msg3;
#                                   both outer wrappers undecryptable
#
# --- Conclusion ---
#
# A passive intruder gains no information beyond the public-key
# infrastructure. All session-specific values (nonces, the grant, the photos)
# remain secret. The protocol achieves perfect passive confidentiality under
# the Dolev-Yao model.
#
# Note: this does NOT mean the protocol is secure against an active intruder.
# For example, an active intruder that intercepts msg2 and re-encrypts the
# inner ciphertext for a different recipient could potentially redirect the
# grant — this is the kind of attack that the extended w3 flow (with idp,
# session IDs, and multiple nonces) is designed to prevent.